WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.901
A kitchen balance comes with six-gram

2
00:00:05.982 --> 00:00:09.470
and nine-gram weights, as many as you like.

3
00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.984
A weight can go on either pan

4
00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:14.092
so the balance weighs six

5
00:00:14.174 --> 00:00:17.580
x plus nine y grams, for integers x and y.

6
00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.008
Can it weigh exactly one

7
00:00:20.094 --> 00:00:22.085
hundred grams of flour?

8
00:00:22.172 --> 00:00:24.682
A computer tries ten thousand

9
00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:27.019
two hundred and one pairs.

10
00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:30.222
It reaches ninety-three, ninety-six

11
00:00:30.222 --> 00:00:33.165
ninety-nine, one hundred and two.

12
00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:34.810
Never one hundred.

13
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.645
But there are infinitely many pairs.

14
00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:41.260
Is one hundred impossible?

15
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.680
Pause and find a reason.

16
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.799
Before the balance, two tools.

17
00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:55.282
Start from a statement: if A, then B.

18
00:00:55.282 --> 00:00:58.821
It is false in only one case

19
00:00:58.821 --> 00:01:01.180
A true and B false.

20
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.656
Its contrapositive says: if not B

21
00:01:04.656 --> 00:01:05.759
then not A.

22
00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:07.690
Compare the columns.

23
00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.989
They agree in every row.

24
00:01:10.081 --> 00:01:12.747
So proving the contrapositive

25
00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:14.770
proves the statement.

26
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.420
The converse, if B then A, is different.

27
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.648
It fails in the third row

28
00:01:20.648 --> 00:01:23.040
where the statement is true.

29
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.170
Here is a theorem where the

30
00:01:26.264 --> 00:01:28.248
contrapositive helps.

31
00:01:28.248 --> 00:01:32.403
If n squared minus six n plus five is even

32
00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:33.820
then n is odd.

33
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.760
Starting from an even value

34
00:01:36.856 --> 00:01:39.158
tells us little about n.

35
00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:43.283
So prove the contrapositive: if n is even

36
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:45.010
the value is odd.

37
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.877
Assume n is even: n equals two a.

38
00:01:48.984 --> 00:01:52.288
Then n squared minus six n plus

39
00:01:52.395 --> 00:01:56.551
five is four a squared, minus twelve a

40
00:01:56.551 --> 00:02:00.921
plus five, which is two times something

41
00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:01.987
plus one.

42
00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:02.520
Odd.

43
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.681
On the number line

44
00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:08.398
every even n gives an odd value: five

45
00:02:08.398 --> 00:02:12.591
minus three, minus three, five, twenty-one

46
00:02:12.591 --> 00:02:13.735
forty-five.

47
00:02:13.735 --> 00:02:16.213
The contrapositive holds

48
00:02:16.213 --> 00:02:18.310
so the theorem holds.

49
00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.438
Now a trap: the converse.

50
00:02:21.438 --> 00:02:25.820
If n is a multiple of six, then n is even.

51
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:26.432
True.

52
00:02:26.432 --> 00:02:29.795
Its contrapositive: if n is odd

53
00:02:29.795 --> 00:02:32.649
n is not a multiple of six.

54
00:02:32.649 --> 00:02:33.770
Also true.

55
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.310
But the converse says: if n is even

56
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.879
then n is a multiple of six.

57
00:02:39.879 --> 00:02:42.360
Is that the same statement?

58
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.652
Try n equals four.

59
00:02:44.652 --> 00:02:45.984
Four is even

60
00:02:45.984 --> 00:02:49.312
but four is not a multiple of six.

61
00:02:49.312 --> 00:02:51.500
The converse is false.

62
00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:55.104
A true statement can have a false converse.

63
00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.829
Proving the converse proves nothing

64
00:02:57.905 --> 00:02:59.418
about the statement.

65
00:02:59.418 --> 00:03:02.370
Only the contrapositive is equivalent.

66
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.515
Back to the balance, with the second tool

67
00:03:06.515 --> 00:03:08.553
proof by contradiction.

68
00:03:08.553 --> 00:03:11.948
To prove B, suppose B is false and show

69
00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.070
that this cannot happen.

70
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.060
So suppose, to the contrary

71
00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:20.455
that some integers x and y give six

72
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.662
x plus nine y equals one hundred.

73
00:03:23.662 --> 00:03:27.905
Take out a three: one hundred is three times

74
00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:29.791
two x plus three y.

75
00:03:29.791 --> 00:03:32.620
So three divides one hundred.

76
00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:36.085
But one hundred sits strictly between

77
00:03:36.169 --> 00:03:39.199
ninety-nine and one hundred and two

78
00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:41.977
two multiples of three in a row.

79
00:03:41.977 --> 00:03:45.176
So three does not divide one hundred.

80
00:03:45.176 --> 00:03:48.290
Both can't be true: a contradiction.

81
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.500
The supposition was false.

82
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.290
No pair of integers gives one hundred

83
00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.080
not just the pairs the computer tried.

84
00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.414
So a proof by contradiction has four steps.

85
00:04:01.414 --> 00:04:03.073
One, assume A.

86
00:04:03.073 --> 00:04:06.500
Two, suppose, to the contrary

87
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.380
that B is false.

88
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.146
Three, argue until some statement and

89
00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:15.285
its negation are both true: here

90
00:04:15.285 --> 00:04:19.348
three divides one hundred, and it does not.

91
00:04:19.348 --> 00:04:22.210
Four, conclude that B is true.

92
00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.394
Try it yourself

93
00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:28.904
if five n plus three is even, then n is odd.

94
00:04:28.904 --> 00:04:31.510
Prove the contrapositive.

95
00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:32.895
In part two

96
00:04:32.895 --> 00:04:36.433
contradiction meets the natural numbers.

97
00:04:36.519 --> 00:04:38.762
Every nonempty set of them

98
00:04:38.848 --> 00:04:40.574
has a least element

99
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.990
so a false claim would have

100
00:04:43.076 --> 00:04:45.060
a least counterexample.
